    is to say the second axis exists; the price list is where it is sold, and
    /pricing renders the same plans() output with the inclusions and the
    build-asserted saving line in full. Two plan grids on one site is one grid
    too many, and the homepage was the copy that would go stale.

    The plans() import went with it. If a plan grid is ever wanted back here,
    import plans() again — do NOT type the figures. */}

{/* ── 14 · PLANNING BACKWARDS ───────────────────────────────────────────
    The urgency band, and the only honest version of one available here: the
    scarcity is real (one person, a capacity gate that already refuses weeks)
    and it is stated as capacity rather than as a countdown. No "3 spots left"
    — that is a number the page cannot verify against the D1 calendar, and a
    scarcity claim a visitor can disprove is worse than none.

    It reads as a service to the reader rather than pressure: a launch date is
    the one date they already have in their head, and this band does the
    subtraction for them. */}
<section class="band-tight">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="statement statement-left hv-pl-head">
      <h2>{c.plH}</h2>
      <p class="lede">{c.plLede}</p>
    </div>
    <ol class="hv-pl">
      {c.pl.map(([t, b], i) => (
        <li>
          <span class="hv-pl-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <h3 class={['w-blue', 'w-violet', 'w-coral'][i] || ''}>{t}</h3>
          <p>{b}</p>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>
    <div class="hv-pl-foot">
      <p>{c.plNote}</p>
      <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={lp('/start')}>{c.plCta}</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

{/* ── 15 · "WHAT THE NUMBERS ACTUALLY ARE" — DELETED ────────────────────
    Four coloured figures, and three of them were already on the page:

      · the floor rate — now the bottom row of the ladder table at section 2,
        where it sits under the four rates above it and shows what "it falls as
        you add" means instead of asserting it;
      · the 48-hour window — the hero plinth's first fact, and the middle step
        of the planning band at 7c, which now carries WINDOW_THRESHOLD too;
      · the test sample price — the promise quote and the closing panel.

    Only "4 house styles: Glow, Dunes, Flash and Phone-made" was said nowhere
    else, and it is a description of what the studio DOES, not a statistic. It
    is in the pipeline at section 4 now, in the step that describes the run.

    A stats band is a summary of the page above it, which is a thing a page
    only needs when it is too long to hold in the head — the fix was the length,
    not a recap. Nothing here was lost; do not restore it as a fifth pricing
    surface. */}

{/* ── 16 · THE OBJECTIONS ───────────────────────────────────────────────
    Four risk questions, answered where the risk is felt rather than two clicks
    away on /faq. The wording is lifted from the answers already in
    src/data/faq.js rather than rewritten, so the homepage cannot end up
    promising something the FAQ page contradicts — the rights answer in
    particular is one a customer could hold the studio to.

    Risk here, logistics in the accordion below: these four are the ones that
    stop a purchase, which is why they get cards and not a summary row. */}
<section class="band">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="statement">
      <h2>{c.obH}</h2>
      <p class="lede">{c.obLede}</p>
    </div>
    <div class="hv-ob">
      {c.ob.map(([q, a, linkText, href]) => (
        <div class="hv-ob-item">
          <h3>{q}</h3>
          <p>{a}</p>
          {linkText && href && (<a class="hv-ob-link" href={lp(href)}>{linkText}</a>)}
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

{/* ── 17 · FAQ ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    Native <details>/<summary>, so it opens with JavaScript off and is
    keyboard-operable without a line of script. Five questions read from
    faq.js — the same objects /faq renders and src/data/schema.js turns into
    FAQPage JSON-LD. No schema is emitted here on purpose: two pages emitting
    FAQPage nodes for the same questions is the duplication faq.js's own header
    warns about. */}
<section class="band-tight hv-faq-band">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <div class="statement">
      <h2>{c.faqH}</h2>
      <p class="lede">{c.faqLede}</p>
    </div>
    <div class="hv-faq">
      {FAQ_PICK.map((item) => (
        <Disclose summary={item.q} tone="panel">
          {item.html
            ? (<div class="hv-faq-a" set:html={item.html} />)
            : (<div class="hv-faq-a"><p>{item.a}</p></div>)}
        </Disclose>
      ))}
    </div>
    <div class="hv-faq-foot">
      <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={lp('/faq')}>{c.faqCta}</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

{/* ── 18 · THE PROMISE ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
    Lit by the WebGL chrome field. .hero-fallback carries the same scene when
    the shader never initialises, which is what makes the material removable
    rather than load-bearing. */}
{/* .ground-blend: the chrome field is a <canvas> filling the section edge to
    edge, so without it the marbled material starts and stops on two hard
    horizontal lines. See the primitive in global.css — it lays --bg-0 back
    over both edges and fades out, and it works on a canvas because it never
    touches the canvas. */}
<section class="ch ch-promise band ground-blend">
  <div class="hero-fallback" aria-hidden="true"></div>
  <canvas id="hero-shader" aria-hidden="true"></canvas>
  <div class="container">
    <div class="hv-promise">
      <div>
        <h2 class="hv-promise-h"><span>{c.promiseH[0]}</span><span class="dim">{c.promiseH[1]}</span></h2>
        <p class="hv-quote">{c.promiseQuote}</p>
        <p class="hv-sign"><strong>Lucas</strong> · {c.promiseSign}</p>
      </div>
      <ul class="hv-checks">
        {c.promiseChecks.map(([t, b]) => (
          <li>
            <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 6L9 17l-5-5" /></svg>
            <span><strong>{t}</strong> — {b}</span>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
    <ul class="hv-trust">{c.trust.map((t) => (<li>{t}</li>))}</ul>
  </div>
</section>

{/* ── 19 · CLOSING ─────────────────────── */}
<section class="band-tall hv-close panel-tonal">
  <div class="container closing">
    <div>
      <h2>{c.closeH}</h2>
      <p class="lede">{c.closeLede}</p>
      <div class="statement actions">
        <a href={lp('/test-sample')} class="btn btn-primary btn-lg">{c.closeCta}</a>
        <a href={lp('/start')} class="btn btn-ghost btn-lg">{c.closeAlt}</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  /* ── hero ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     100svh, not 100vh: on iOS the URL bar makes vh taller than the visible
     area, so a "full height" hero puts its own bottom edge under the browser
     chrome and the plinth is the thing that disappears. */
  /* padding-block is reset explicitly: global.css gives every <section> a
     clamp(4.5rem, 9vw, 9rem) block padding, and on a full-bleed hero that
     lands as 130px of dead photograph under the plinth — measured, not
     guessed. A hero is not a band and does not take a band's rhythm. */
  .hv-hero { position: relative; min-height: 100svh; display: flex; flex-direction: column; overflow: hidden; margin-top: -76px; padding: 76px 0 0; isolation: isolate; background: var(--ink-900); }
  .hv-hero-bg { position: absolute; inset: 0; z-index: -2; }
  .hv-hero-bg img { width: 100%; height: 100%; object-fit: cover; object-position: center 26%; }
  /* Two layers doing two jobs. The radial is the plate under the headline;
     the linear darkens the band the sticky nav stands on and lifts the foot
     so the plinth reads as a solid rail rather than as a floating strip. */
  .hv-hero-scrim { position: absolute; inset: 0; z-index: -1; background:
    radial-gradient(92% 82% at 16% 84%, rgb(var(--scrim) / 0.90) 0%, rgb(var(--scrim) / 0.42) 54%, transparent 78%),
    linear-gradient(180deg, rgb(var(--scrim) / 0.76) 0%, rgb(var(--scrim) / 0.50) 13%, rgb(var(--scrim) / 0.14) 27%, transparent 52%, rgb(var(--scrim) / 0.46) 84%, rgb(var(--scrim) / 0.66) 100%); }
  .hv-hero-inner { flex: 1; display: flex; align-items: flex-end; width: 100%; }
  .hv-hero-copy { max-width: 40rem; padding-block: 3rem clamp(2.5rem, 6vh, 4rem); }
  /* 900, NOT 800 — Lucas: *"misschien kan je de witte tekst dikgedrukt maken."*
     The complaint underneath it was that "visualized." looks bigger than the
     two lines above. It is not: all three run at the same font-size and their
     x-heights are within 1% of each other. What differs is WEIGHT — the italic
     line's stems are 73px against the white lines' 26px at a 200px test size,
     2.8 times thicker — and mass reads as size.

     Big Shoulders at its maximum only closes part of that: 900 takes the stem
     from 26px to 30px, so the italic is still 2.4x thicker. That is the price
     of a condensed display face, whose stems stay narrow at every weight by
     construction. It is worth doing anyway — the perceived gap narrows much
     more than the stem ratio suggests, because at display size the counters
     close up too — but the two faces will never weigh the same, and chasing
     that with font-size would make the line genuinely smaller while still
     looking heavier. */
  .hv-h1 { font-family: var(--font-heading); font-weight: 900; font-size: var(--t-hero); line-height: 0.98; letter-spacing: -0.012em; margin: 0; color: var(--paper-on-dark); }
  .hv-line { display: block; }
  /* :global(), omdat deze <em> via set:html binnenkomt en dus geen
     data-astro-cid-attribuut draagt — precies dezelfde reden als bij
     `.pack-p :global(a)` in CatalogPage.astro. Zonder dit blijft het accent wit. */
  .hv-h1 :global(em) { font-family: var(--font-editorial); font-style: italic; font-weight: 900; letter-spacing: -0.03em; }
  .hv-lede { margin: clamp(1.2rem, 2.4vw, 1.9rem) 0 0; max-width: 46ch; font-size: var(--t-lg); line-height: 1.55; color: var(--paper-soft-on-dark); }
  .hv-cta { display: flex; gap: var(--gap-actions); flex-wrap: wrap; margin-top: clamp(1.6rem, 3vw, 2.2rem); }
  @media (max-width: 560px) { .hv-cta > .btn { width: 100%; } }

  /* The proof line. No ground of its own — it sits on the photograph, lit by
     the hero scrim that was already there for the headline.

     The two coloured underscores went with the bar. They were doing a job the
     bar created: three columns of identical grey needed something to say which
     fact mattered, so a delivery window got an amber rule and a review claim a
     green one. A checked list needs neither — the tick IS the emphasis, and
     three of them in the accent is one signal repeated rather than two signals
     competing. Colour spent on a decoration is colour not available for the
     button six pixels above it.

     align-items: flex-start on the row, not center: the third line wraps to
     two on a laptop and a centred tick would then float beside the gap between
     them instead of marking the first word. */
  /* STACKED, not a wrapping row, at every width. Laid out as a row it fell
     2 + 1 on a 1440px screen — the copy column is capped at 40rem and the
     three lines total wider than that — and a list that wraps unevenly reads
     as an accident rather than as a list. Three ticks down the left margin is
     also the shape a reader already knows: it is a checklist, which is exactly
     the claim being made. */
  .hv-proof { list-style: none; margin: clamp(1.3rem, 2.6vw, 1.9rem) 0 0; padding: 0;
    display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: .5rem; }
  .hv-proof li { display: flex; align-items: flex-start; gap: .5rem;
    font-size: .9rem; line-height: 1.4; color: var(--paper-soft-on-dark); }
  .hv-proof svg { flex: 0 0 auto; width: 1.05em; height: 1.05em; margin-top: .16em;
    fill: none; stroke: var(--accent); stroke-width: 2.6; stroke-linecap: round; stroke-linejoin: round; }

  @media (max-width: 760px) {
    /* The WhatsApp launcher is position: fixed at bottom-left and the hero is
       100svh, so on a phone the launcher and the foot of the hero occupy the
       same pixels on first paint — it used to sit on top of the third fact in
       the plinth and it would sit on the third proof line now. 48px launcher +
       its 12px inset + air. Measured, not padded by feel. */
    .hv-hero-copy { padding-bottom: 5.25rem; }
  }

  /* ── the one accent word ────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Teal is spent on the primary action and one headline word. On the dark
     ground --teal-text is unreadable, so the highlight is carried by full
     paper against the soft paper the rest of the line runs at — value rather
     than hue, which is the same idea in the material the ground allows. */
  /* The accent word in a section heading. It was paper-on-paper — the same
     white as the rest of the line, so "accent" meant italics-turned-off and
     nothing else. It is the palette's blue now, which is what OPP does with
     the word that carries the sentence. */
  .hv-accent { font-style: normal; color: var(--fill-blue); }


  /* ── tiles ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .hv-tile-sub { margin: .45rem 0 0; font-size: .9rem; color: var(--ink-2); }
  .hv-worked-tiles, .hv-svc-tiles { margin-top: clamp(1.6rem, 3vw, 2.4rem); }

  /* ── the price band ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     THE ONE VAT LINE. It is a block qualifier, so it has to read as governing
     what comes after it rather than as the end of the lede — hence the rule
     above it and the uppercase tracking, which is the same treatment the
     .lad-kind-vat cell gets on /pricing — minus its uppercase, because that
     cell is two words and this is a sentence, and a sentence in caps shouts.
     It sits between the statement and the table on purpose: a qualifier under
     the figures is a footnote, and a footnote is what "excl. VAT" ×8 was
     trying to avoid being. */
  .hv-price-vat {
    margin: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 3rem) 0 0;
    padding-top: clamp(1rem, 2vw, 1.4rem);
    box-shadow: inset 0 1px 0 var(--line);
    font-size: .82rem; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: .01em; color: var(--ink-2);
  }
  /* The rate card. Radius on the wrapper rather than the table, because a
     table cannot clip its own corners and --r-md is the step this system gives
     a surface inside a band. overflow-x so four columns survive a 320px
     screen without the page itself scrolling sideways. */
  .hv-lad-wrap {
    margin-top: clamp(1rem, 2vw, 1.4rem);
    border: 1px solid var(--line-strong);
    border-radius: var(--r-md);
    overflow-x: auto;
  }
  /* Lining, tabular: rates in a column have to line up or the column stops
     being scannable, which is the whole reason this is a table. */
  .hv-lad { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums lining-nums; }
  .hv-lad th, .hv-lad td { padding: clamp(.7rem, 1.4vw, 1rem) clamp(.7rem, 1.6vw, 1.3rem); text-align: right; vertical-align: top; }
  .hv-lad thead th { vertical-align: bottom; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line-strong); }
  .hv-lad tbody tr + tr th, .hv-lad tbody tr + tr td { border-top: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .hv-lad thead th:first-child, .hv-lad tbody th { text-align: left; }
  .hv-lad-corner { font-size: .68rem; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: .08em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-3); white-space: nowrap; }
  .hv-lad-kind { display: block; font-size: .95rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--ink); }
  /* A link in a table header needs a cue AT REST — nobody hovers five columns
     to find out which two are clickable. Hairline underline, accented on
     interaction, so the page's accent stays on the CTA. */
  .hv-lad-kind a { text-decoration: underline; text-decoration-thickness: 1px; text-decoration-color: var(--line-strong); text-underline-offset: 4px; }
  .hv-lad-kind a:hover, .hv-lad-kind a:focus-visible { text-decoration-thickness: 2px; text-decoration-color: var(--accent); }
  .hv-lad-sub { display: block; margin-top: .3rem; font-size: .74rem; font-weight: 400; line-height: 1.4; color: var(--ink-3); }
  .hv-lad tbody th { font-family: var(--font-display); font-weight: 700; font-size: .95rem; color: var(--ink); white-space: nowrap; }
  .hv-lad td { font-family: var(--font-display); font-weight: 800; font-size: clamp(1rem, 1.6vw, 1.25rem); line-height: 1; letter-spacing: -0.02em; color: var(--ink); }
  .hv-lad-note { margin: 1rem 0 0; font-size: .85rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--ink-3); max-width: 62ch; }
  .hv-worked-h { margin: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 3rem) 0 0; font-size: var(--t-h3); font-weight: 700; color: var(--ink); }
  /* The discount, on its own line with a rule above it, because it is a
     condition on every figure in the band and not a fourth worked example. */
  .hv-price-first {
    margin: clamp(1.6rem, 3vw, 2.2rem) 0 0;
    padding-top: clamp(1.1rem, 2vw, 1.5rem);
    box-shadow: inset 0 1px 0 var(--line);
    font-size: .9rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--ink-3); max-width: 66ch;
  }
  .hv-price-first strong { color: var(--ink); font-weight: 700; }
  .hv-price-foot { margin-top: clamp(1.4rem, 2.6vw, 2rem); display: flex; align-items: flex-start; justify-content: space-between; gap: clamp(1.2rem, 3vw, 2.5rem); flex-wrap: wrap; }
  .hv-price-notes { flex: 1 1 28rem; display: grid; gap: .7rem; }
  .hv-price-notes p { margin: 0; max-width: 62ch; font-size: .85rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--ink-3); }
    /* `flex: 0 0 auto` refused to shrink, so at 360px the Dutch ghost button
     ("De volledige prijslijst", 174px against the English 138px) pushed the row
     to 370.8px and the whole page scrolled sideways. It may shrink now; the
     buttons wrap instead. */
  .hv-price-cta { flex: 1 1 auto; min-width: 0; display: flex; gap: .75rem; flex-wrap: wrap; }
  /* vatNote() as a disclosure rather than as small print. It is forty words
     that matter enormously to the one reader in ten who is about to check
     whether reverse charge applies, and are noise to the other nine — which is
     exactly what <details> is for, and the pattern /faq and /start already use.
     The label above still says "excl. VAT" in plain sight; this is only the
     mechanism. Do not inline it back: that is how a price band grows a
     paragraph of tax law between the rate card and the button. */
  .hv-vat > summary {
    list-style: none; cursor: pointer;
    display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: .5rem;
    font-size: .82rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--ink-2);
  }
  .hv-vat > summary::-webkit-details-marker { display: none; }
  .hv-vat > summary::before { content: '+'; font-family: var(--font-display); font-weight: 900; color: var(--ink-3); }
  .hv-vat[open] > summary::before { content: '–'; }
  .hv-vat > summary:hover { color: var(--accent); }
  .hv-vat p { margin: .6rem 0 0; max-width: 62ch; font-size: .82rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--ink-3); }

  /* ── the services ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     A whole-tile link, so the target is the card and not a word inside it.
     Radii are inherited: .tile carries --r-lg and clips its own media, which
     is why nothing here sets a corner except the inset .hv-svc-get plate —
     that one is a surface of its own and takes --r-md, the step below the
     card that contains it. Nothing in this band is allowed a hard corner. */
  .hv-svc-tile { color: inherit; transition: background var(--dur-1) var(--ease); }
  .hv-svc-tile:hover { background: var(--bg-raise); }
  /* One aspect ratio, now that all four tiles are one width. 16/9 rather than
     the 4/3 the narrow tiles used to take: a 4/3 crop inside a half-row tile
     is 480px of photograph before a word is read, and on a phone — where the
     four stack — that was most of what made this band four viewports tall. */
  .hv-svc-media { aspect-ratio: 16 / 9; }
  .hv-svc-media img { transition: transform var(--dur-3) var(--ease); }
  .hv-svc-tile:hover .hv-svc-media img { transform: scale(1.03); }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .hv-svc-media img, .hv-svc-tile:hover .hv-svc-media img { transition: none; transform: none; }
  }
  .hv-svc-body { gap: 0; }
  .hv-svc-line { font-size: .875rem; line-height: 1.55; color: var(--ink-3); }
  .hv-svc-get {
    margin-top: 1rem;
    padding: .7rem .85rem;
    background: var(--surface-2);
    border-radius: var(--r-md);
    font-size: .82rem; line-height: 1.5; color: var(--ink-2);
  }
  /* .hv-svc-price and .hv-svc-get's "figures line up across tiles" rule came
     out with the prices — see the band's own comment. The inset plate above is
     now the last thing in a tile, and it does not need a floor. */
  .hv-svc-foot { margin-top: clamp(1.6rem, 3vw, 2.4rem); }

  @media (max-width: 700px) {
    .rail-nav { display: none; }
    /* Two cards fit inside 390px, separated by the rail's 1px rule — which
       put one item's caption hard against the next item's label with nothing
       between them but a hairline. One and a bit cards is the right number on
       a phone: it shows there is more without pretending to show it. The
       caption stacks as well, because a label and a note side by side inside
       a 15rem card is two columns of two words. */
    .rail > * { flex-basis: 74vw; }
    .plate-cap { flex-direction: column; gap: .15rem; }
  }

  /* ── pipeline ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .hv-pipe-head { max-width: 40rem; }
  .hv-pipe { list-style: none; margin: clamp(2.4rem, 5vw, 3.5rem) 0 0; padding: 0; display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(15rem, 1fr)); gap: 1px; background: var(--line); }
  .hv-pipe > li { background: var(--bg); padding: clamp(1.25rem, 2.2vw, 1.9rem); }
  /* Outlined numerals: the colour is the STROKE, because a filled 2.4rem 900
     numeral in four hues would out-shout the step it labels. The four are
     7.91–8.07:1 on this ground, well past the 3:1 a large glyph needs. */
  .hv-pipe-n { font-family: var(--font-display); font-weight: 900; font-size: 2.4rem; line-height: 1; color: transparent; -webkit-text-stroke: 1.5px var(--line-ui); display: block; }
  .hv-pipe-n.s-blue   { -webkit-text-stroke-color: var(--fill-blue); }
  .hv-pipe-n.s-violet { -webkit-text-stroke-color: var(--fill-violet); }
  .hv-pipe-n.s-pink   { -webkit-text-stroke-color: var(--fill-pink); }
  .hv-pipe-n.s-coral  { -webkit-text-stroke-color: var(--fill-coral); }
  .hv-pipe h3 { margin: 1rem 0 .45rem; font-size: 1.02rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--ink); }
  .hv-pipe p { margin: 0; font-size: .875rem; line-height: 1.55; color: var(--ink-3); }

  /* The .hv-vs* rules went with the "What an order replaces" band — see the
     note where that band used to be, above section 6. */

  /* ── the dashboard band ────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .hv-dash { margin-top: clamp(2.6rem, 5vw, 4rem); display: grid; gap: 1px; background: var(--line); }
  .hv-dash-row { background: var(--bg); display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 5fr) minmax(0, 7fr); gap: clamp(1.5rem, 3vw, 3rem); align-items: center; padding: clamp(1.5rem, 3vw, 2.6rem); }
  .hv-dash-row.is-flip .hv-dash-copy { order: 2; }
  .hv-dash-copy h3 { margin: 0 0 .7rem; font-size: var(--t-h3); font-weight: 700; color: var(--ink); }
  .hv-dash-copy p { margin: 0 0 1.3rem; font-size: .95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--ink-2); max-width: 46ch; }
  .hv-dash-log { background: var(--bg); padding: clamp(1.5rem, 3vw, 2.6rem); }
  .hv-dash-log h3 { margin: 0 0 .7rem; font-size: var(--t-h3); font-weight: 700; color: var(--ink); }
  .hv-dash-log p { margin: 0 0 1.3rem; font-size: .95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--ink-2); max-width: 68ch; }
  @media (max-width: 820px) {
    .hv-dash-row { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .hv-dash-row.is-flip .hv-dash-copy { order: 0; }
  }

  /* The .hv-stat* rules went with the "What the numbers actually are" band —
     see the note where that band used to be, above section 8b. */

  /* ── the promise ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .hv-promise { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(20rem, 1fr)); gap: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 4rem); align-items: start; }
  .hv-promise-h { font-size: var(--t-h2); line-height: 1.05; letter-spacing: -0.035em; margin: 0; }
  .hv-promise-h span { display: block; }
  .hv-promise-h .dim { color: var(--ink-3); }
  .hv-quote { font-family: var(--font-editorial); font-style: italic; font-weight: 500; font-size: clamp(1.15rem, 1.9vw, 1.45rem); line-height: 1.45; color: var(--ink); margin: 1.4rem 0 0; max-width: 40ch; }
  .hv-sign { margin: 1rem 0 0; font-size: .875rem; color: var(--ink-3); }
  .hv-sign strong { color: var(--ink-2); }
  .hv-checks { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; display: grid; gap: 1.1rem; }
  .hv-checks li { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 20px 1fr; gap: .75rem; font-size: .9rem; line-height: 1.55; color: var(--ink-3); }
  .hv-checks strong { color: var(--ink); }
  .hv-checks svg { width: 18px; height: 18px; margin-top: .18rem; fill: none; stroke: var(--ink-2); stroke-width: 2; }
  .hv-trust { list-style: none; display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: .5rem 1.8rem; margin: clamp(2.2rem, 4vw, 3rem) 0 0; padding: clamp(1.4rem, 2.5vw, 2rem) 0 0; box-shadow: inset 0 1px 0 var(--line); font-size: .82rem; color: var(--ink-3); }

  /* ── who it is for ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .hv-who-tiles { margin-top: clamp(2.4rem, 5vw, 4rem); }
  /* The index is a label, not a step: the four situations are alternatives, so
     it is set small and coloured rather than drawn as the stroked numerals the
     pipeline uses — those mean sequence, and reusing them here would promise an
     order that does not exist. */
  .hv-who-i { display: block; font-family: var(--font-display); font-weight: 900; font-size: .74rem; letter-spacing: .14em; margin-bottom: 1.1rem; }
  /* A <details>, so the grid that used to put the label beside the list is
     gone: a disclosure has to open downwards or the summary stops looking like
     a control. The rule above it stays — it is what separates the four
     situations from the admission under them. */
  .hv-notfor { margin-top: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 3rem); padding-top: clamp(1.4rem, 2.5vw, 2rem); box-shadow: inset 0 1px 0 var(--line); }
  .hv-notfor-h {
    margin: 0; list-style: none; cursor: pointer;
    display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: .5rem;
    font-size: .72rem; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: .1em;
    text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-3);
  }
  .hv-notfor-h::-webkit-details-marker { display: none; }
  .hv-notfor-h::after { content: '+'; font-family: var(--font-display); font-weight: 900; letter-spacing: 0; }
  .hv-notfor[open] .hv-notfor-h::after { content: '–'; }
  .hv-notfor-h:hover { color: var(--ink-2); }
  .hv-notfor ul { list-style: none; margin: 1.1rem 0 0; padding: 0; display: grid; gap: .65rem; max-width: 62ch; }
  .hv-notfor li { position: relative; padding-left: 1.4rem; font-size: .9rem; line-height: 1.55; color: var(--ink-3); }
  /* Coral, not the blue accent: this is the one list on the page that is a
     warning rather than an offer, and the palette already reserves coral for
     the figure that is being counted against you. */
  .hv-notfor li::before { content: ''; position: absolute; left: 0; top: .64em; width: 9px; height: 1px; background: var(--fill-coral); }

  /* ── before / after ────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .hv-ba-head { max-width: 44rem; }
  .hv-ba { margin-top: clamp(2.2rem, 4.5vw, 3.4rem); display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 5fr) minmax(0, 6fr); gap: clamp(1.6rem, 4vw, 3.4rem); align-items: center; }
  /* :global because .cmp is rendered inside Compare.astro and carries that
     component's scope attribute, not this one's. */
  .hv-ba-media :global(.cmp) { border-radius: var(--r-lg); }
  .hv-ba-points { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; }
  .hv-ba-points li { padding: clamp(1rem, 2vw, 1.4rem) 0; }
  .hv-ba-points li:first-child { padding-top: 0; }
  .hv-ba-points li + li { box-shadow: inset 0 1px 0 var(--line); }
  .hv-ba-points h3 { margin: 0 0 .4rem; font-size: 1.02rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--ink); }
  .hv-ba-points p { margin: 0; font-size: .9rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--ink-3); }
  .hv-ba-note { margin: clamp(1.2rem, 2.4vw, 1.8rem) 0 0; padding-top: clamp(1.1rem, 2vw, 1.5rem); box-shadow: inset 0 1px 0 var(--line); font-size: .85rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--ink-3); }
  .hv-ba-note a { text-decoration: underline; text-decoration-thickness: 1px; text-underline-offset: 3px; text-decoration-color: var(--line-strong); white-space: nowrap; }
  .hv-ba-note a:hover { text-decoration-color: var(--accent); }
  @media (max-width: 900px) { .hv-ba { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }

  /* ── the dream ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  /* The page's second full-bleed photograph, and the only band with nothing to
     count. The scrim is horizontal rather than the hero's vertical one so the
     photograph stays open on the right where there is no copy over it — the
     picture is the argument, the text just names it. */
  .hv-dream { position: relative; isolation: isolate; overflow: hidden; display: grid; align-items: center; min-height: min(84svh, 44rem); padding-block: clamp(4.5rem, 10vw, 8rem); background: var(--ink-900); }
  .hv-dream-bg { position: absolute; inset: 0; z-index: -2; width: 100%; height: 100%; object-fit: cover; object-position: center 28%; }
  .hv-dream-scrim { position: absolute; inset: 0; z-index: -1; background:
